\begin_layout Standard
The 1998 Gregor Weihs dataset was groundbreaking for its time,
 but the three celebrated 2015 "loophole-free" Bell tests are much cleaner because they simultaneously closed the locality and detection loopholes without forcing fair-sampling assumptions.
 [1] (https://encyclopedia.pub/entry/36569)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The official public raw data repositories for the 2015 loophole-free tests can be found at the following permanent archives:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 The Delft University (Hanson Lab) 2015 Dataset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This was the historic first experiment that used entangled electron spins in diamonds separated by 1.3 kilometres across the TU Delft campus.
 [1] (https://research.tudelft.nl/en/datasets/loophole-free-bell-inequality-violation-using-electron-spins-sepa),
 [2] (https://qutech.nl/lab/hanson-lab/research-highlights/hanson-lab-loophole-free-bell-test/)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Where to get it:
 The full raw dataset is publicly indexed and downloadable from the TU Delft 4TU.ResearchData Repository.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Structure:
 It includes clear plaintext or binary text tracks containing the time-stamped basis selections (the active settings) and the successful spin readout counts.
 Because there were only 245 total trials in this run,
 it is the perfect dataset for a small sample size verification inside your VS Code environment.
 [1] (https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/674004/availability-of-dataset-from-loophole-free-bell-inequality-violation-using-elec),
 [2] (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26503041/),
 [3] (https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.05949),
 [4] (https://encyclopedia.pub/entry/36569)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 The Vienna (Giustina et al.
 / Zeilinger Group) 2015 Dataset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment used high-efficiency superconducting sheet detectors placed deep inside the cellars of the Hofburg Palace in Vienna,
 using polarization-entangled photons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Where to get it:
 The raw timestamp records are hosted via the IQOQI Vienna (Institute for Quantum Optics and Quantum Information) Data Portal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Structure:
 This layout is highly compatible with the logic you just wrote.
 It uses time-taggers to record raw channels for the reference clock,
 the fast active random-number generators,
 and the single-photon detector firings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 The NIST (Shalm et al.) 2015 Dataset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the photon-based experiment conducted at the Boulder labs in Colorado.
 The data files you are currently using—
including your ClassicalRNGXOR run—
belong to the broader sub-folders of this specific experiment.
 [1] (https://academics.hamilton.edu/physics/smajor/courses/135Guides/PhysRevLett.115.250401.pdf),
 [2] (https://encyclopedia.pub/entry/36569)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Where to get it:
 The parent repository containing both the classical control runs and the active quantum-basis runs is hosted directly on the NIST Bell Test Research Data Repository.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
• Finding the Active Angle Runs:
 To find the files where the Pockels cells are actively rotating the angles to test the actual CHSH boundaries (rather than staying parallel like the control run),
 look in the file manifest for the runs labeled QuantumRNG or EberhardBasis,
 such as the active run.alice.dat bundles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Preparing Your Ada Code for the New Files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since you are returning to your old data notes or downloading the Delft/Vienna files,
 your Ada sliding-pointer algorithm is already 95% prepared to handle them.
 You will only need to make two minor adjustments in your VS Code text files:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 The Time Unit Scaling:
 Check whether the new files register their Time_Tag values in nanoseconds,
 picoseconds,
 or hardware clock ticks (for example,
 the NIST Tagger uses custom 77.45-picosecond bins).
 You will adjust your tight threshold := 20;
 constraint to match their time scale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 The Channel IDs:
 Map their local column positions or binary byte-flags directly into your Channel_Type structure.
 [1] (https://rakts-research.org/nist_bell_test.html)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Your Ada program has already proven its mathematical accuracy by identifying hardware asymmetries and sorting parallel coincidences perfectly.
 Now you can feed it a rotating basis dataset to watch how the counts distribute across a dynamic geometry.
\end_layout

